  "question": {
    "text": "Let $R=\\{(3,3)(5,5),(9,9),(12,12),(5,12),(3,9)$, $(3,12),(3,5)\\}$ be a relation on the $\\operatorname{set} A=\\{3,5,9,12\\}$. Then, $R$ is :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\nreflexive, symmetric but not transitive.\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\nsymmetric, transitive but not reflexive.\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\nan equivalence relation.\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\nreflexive, transitive but not symmetric.",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $\\mathrm{R}=\\{(3,3),(5,5),(9,9),(12,12),(5,12)$, $(3,9),(3,12),(3,5)\\}$ be a relation on set $\\mathrm{A}=\\{3,5,9,12\\}$\nClearly, every element of $A$ is related to itself. Therefore, it is a reflexive.\nNow, $\\mathrm{R}$ is not symmetry because 3 is related to 5 but 5 is not related to 3 .\nAlso $\\mathrm{R}$ is transitive relation because it satisfies the property that if $a \\mathrm{R} b$ and $b \\mathrm{R} c$ then $a \\mathrm{R} c$."
  }
}